   "about": [
    {
     "title": "Why this is not part of journey A",
     "html": "<p>The same people move from A to B at day 90, but the builder sets re-entry per journey: A runs once, B repeats twice a year. B also holds a large backlog of older clients that must be released in batches, which needs its own segment.</p>"
    },
    {
     "title": "Notes on the steps",
     "html": "<p>S04 is also used in journey A. If Voyager counts “once ever” or “every N days” per template rather than per step, give B its own copy of the S04 templates.</p>"
    },
    {
     "title": "New copy",
     "html": "<h4>S05-E1-B — subject-line variant for the A/B test at B5</h4>\n<p>Same body as S05-E1.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Subject:</b> A smaller first step: the Cent account</li><li><b>Preheader:</b> Live markets, with positions 100 times smaller.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
     "title": "Launch",
     "html": "<p>The backlog is large: on 2026-10-03 there were 163,389 clients who had never deposited, most of them registered long ago, and the verified share is not known. Release it one country group at a time, watching bounce and complaint rates between batches:</p>\n<ol><li>Countries other than Nigeria, Kenya and South Africa.</li><li>South Africa, then Kenya.</li><li>Nigeria in two or three parts. Each date filter takes one mode, so for these batches switch Registered from “Over 90 days ago” to a “Date range” covering one registration year.</li></ol>\n<p>When the backlog has gone through, remove the Countries filter and set Registered back to “Over 90 days ago”; new day-90 clients then flow in continuously from journey A.</p>"
    }
   ],
   "variants": {
    "S05-E1-B": {
     "subject": "A smaller first step: the Cent account",
     "preheader": "Live markets, with positions 100 times smaller."
    }
   }
