     "id": "birthday",
     "title": "Birthday & horoscope",
     "docTitle": "Birthday and horoscope email — a daily blast with a 7-day coupon",
     "sub": "366 daily blasts, 12 samples",
     "kind": "birthday",
     "file": "content/email/blasts/birthday-horoscope.md",
     "html": "<p>Every client gets a birthday email on their birthday: a happy-birthday message, a light-hearted “trading personality” card for their star sign, and a 20% coupon valid for seven days. It is the one email a year that is purely friendly, and the coupon gives it a reason to act.</p>\n<h3>How it runs</h3>\n<ul><li><b>One blast for every day of the year</b> (366, including 29 February). Each day’s blast uses the template for that day’s star sign and names the date.</li><li><b>Audience:</b> SEG-16, Birth date: Anniversary in 0 days · Last login: Last 365 days. The login filter keeps the query fast (the anniversary filter timed out on the full base) and avoids mailing people who have long gone quiet.</li><li><b>Time:</b> 07:00 UTC. A later version could send at 09:00 in each client’s time zone.</li><li><b>Why blasts and not one journey:</b> a journey cannot choose a template by date, and Voyager’s date conditions cannot test day and month only. A scheduled blast per day, with the audience evaluated on the day, does both.</li><li><b>Creating 366 blasts:</b> in batches, one month ahead (about 30 at a time). By hand this takes about two hours a month; with the Voyager API or the Claude connector proposed in the PanUI backlog it becomes one request.</li><li><b>29 February birthdays:</b> the 29 February blast runs in leap years; in other years those clients are added to the 28 February audience.</li></ul>\n<h3>The coupon</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Offer</td><td>20% off the fee of one funded-account challenge (TenEdge or TenExpress)</td></tr><tr><td>Valid</td><td>7 days, from the birthday to 23:59 UTC on the seventh day</td></tr><tr><td>Use</td><td>Once per client</td></tr><tr><td>Code</td><td>Unique per client (<code>{{birthday_code}}</code>), generated by the checkout</td></tr><tr><td>Not</td><td>A deposit bonus, cash, or credit on a trading account</td></tr><tr><td>Terms page</td><td>Required on tentrade.com before the first send</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Why a challenge fee and not a deposit bonus.</b> TenTrade’s site offers no deposit bonus, and the review found a template promising one. A discount on a purchase is simpler to explain, easier to cap, and does not tie the birthday to depositing more money into a trading account. The brand decides; this is our recommendation.</p>\n<p><b>Dependencies.</b> Unique codes need the checkout to create them and Voyager to insert them as a merge field. Until both exist, a fallback is one code per day (for example <code>BDAY-1410</code>) that the checkout accepts only from clients whose birthday is that day.</p>\n<h3>The trading personality cards</h3>\n<p>Each sign has a card: the sign’s symbol, a nickname for the kind of trader it jokes about, a one-line joke, and four tongue-in-cheek “stats”. They are written to make people smile and share them, not to describe anyone’s real trading.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Sign</th><th>Dates</th><th>Personality</th><th>The joke</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Capricorn</td><td>22 Dec – 19 Jan</td><td>The Long-Game Climber</td><td>Thinks in quarters. Calendar quarters.</td></tr><tr><td>Aquarius</td><td>20 Jan – 18 Feb</td><td>The Indicator Inventor</td><td>Has customised a chart until nobody else can read it.</td></tr><tr><td>Pisces</td><td>19 Feb – 20 Mar</td><td>The Intuitive Dreamer</td><td>Feels the market’s mood, then sensibly checks the data.</td></tr><tr><td>Aries</td><td>21 Mar – 19 Apr</td><td>The Opening-Bell Sprinter</td><td>Was ready before the chart finished loading.</td></tr><tr><td>Taurus</td><td>20 Apr – 20 May</td><td>The Comfort-Zone Bull</td><td>Treats a trading plan like a favourite restaurant: same table, every time.</td></tr><tr><td>Gemini</td><td>21 May – 20 Jun</td><td>The Twelve-Tab Analyst</td><td>Two monitors, four charts and a strong opinion on each.</td></tr><tr><td>Cancer</td><td>21 Jun – 22 Jul</td><td>The Safe-Haven Keeper</td><td>Checks the economic calendar the way others check the weather.</td></tr><tr><td>Leo</td><td>23 Jul – 22 Aug</td><td>The Leaderboard Lion</td><td>Would like the chart to know who it’s dealing with.</td></tr><tr><td>Virgo</td><td>23 Aug – 22 Sep</td><td>The Spreadsheet Sentinel</td><td>Trading journal colour-coded. Twice.</td></tr><tr><td>Libra</td><td>23 Sep – 22 Oct</td><td>The Risk-Reward Balancer</td><td>Won’t start until everything is balanced, including the desk.</td></tr><tr><td>Scorpio</td><td>23 Oct – 21 Nov</td><td>The Quiet Strategist</td><td>Plans the exit before the entry, and tells no one.</td></tr><tr><td>Sagittarius</td><td>22 Nov – 21 Dec</td><td>The Globe-Trotting Explorer</td><td>Has studied a currency pair mostly to plan the holiday.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4>Rules for the horoscope content</h4>\n<ul><li><b>No trading advice, ever.</b> Nothing connects a star sign to a market, an instrument, a direction, a time to trade or an amount. The cards joke about habits and personalities only.</li><li><b>No predictions.</b> “This year the stars favour…” is never used.</li><li><b>Every email says so:</b> “Horoscopes are just for fun. Nothing in this email is trading advice.”</li><li><b>The coupon is the only offer</b>, with its terms link, and the standard risk warning stays in the footer.</li></ul>\n<h4>Images</h4>\n<p>The cards in the hub are SVG drawings made for review. For sending, each card is exported as a PNG (600 × 400 pixels; most email apps do not show SVG) and hosted with the templates. A designer or an image model can redraw them in TenTrade’s brand style from the same brief: symbol, nickname, joke, four stats.</p>\n<h3>Sample days</h3>\n<p>Twelve days across the year, one per sign, are written out in full in set S24.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Sign</th><th>Email</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>8 January</td><td>Capricorn</td><td>S24-E01</td></tr><tr><td>2 February</td><td>Aquarius</td><td>S24-E02</td></tr><tr><td>6 March</td><td>Pisces</td><td>S24-E03</td></tr><tr><td>5 April</td><td>Aries</td><td>S24-E04</td></tr><tr><td>8 May</td><td>Taurus</td><td>S24-E05</td></tr><tr><td>10 June</td><td>Gemini</td><td>S24-E06</td></tr><tr><td>4 July</td><td>Cancer</td><td>S24-E07</td></tr><tr><td>12 August</td><td>Leo</td><td>S24-E08</td></tr><tr><td>9 September</td><td>Virgo</td><td>S24-E09</td></tr><tr><td>14 October</td><td>Libra</td><td>S24-E10</td></tr><tr><td>7 November</td><td>Scorpio</td><td>S24-E11</td></tr><tr><td>3 December</td><td>Sagittarius</td><td>S24-E12</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The other 354 days use the same sign template with their own date. The hub’s calendar shows every day and its sign.</p>\n<h3>What we measure</h3>\n<p>Open and click rates (we expect them to beat the campaign average), coupon redemptions within the seven days, challenge purchases, and unsubscribes (which should stay at or below normal).</p>",
